{
  "Title": "Onion Creek Flashiness Analysis: Sequence\u2010to\u2010One LSTM Modeling of Daily Stream Discharge from a Seven\u2010Day Precipitation Window (2015\u20132020)",
  "Abstract": "Abstract  \nAccurate forecasting of daily stream discharge is essential for understanding hydrological flashiness in small watersheds. We developed a sequence\u2010to\u2010one long short\u2010term memory (LSTM) model to predict next\u2010day discharge at USGS station 08158000 using antecedent precipitation over a seven\u2010day sliding window. Daily discharge data (parameter code 00060) were obtained from the USGS Water Services API, and precipitation records were fetched from the NOAA NCEI API. After cleaning, interpolation, and alignment, the dataset was partitioned into training (2015\u20132018), validation (2019), and test (2020) subsets. The LSTM architecture, implemented in TensorFlow Keras with an input shape of (7, 1), was trained for 50 epochs using the Adam optimizer and mean squared error loss. On the validation set, the model reached a root\u2010mean\u2010square error (RMSE) of 4450.8 cfs (training RMSE = 4794.8 cfs), with performance assessed by Nash\u2013Sutcliffe Efficiency (NSE) and RMSE via the hydrostats package. To address non\u2010Gaussian discharge distributions, a second LSTM was trained on log\u2010transformed discharge (offset + 0.1), which improved convergence (final validation loss = 1.4669 in log\u2010units). Both models captured key discharge dynamics in Onion Creek, demonstrating the efficacy of LSTM\u2010based approaches for streamflow prediction using limited precipitation inputs.",
  "Keywords": "Keywords: Onion Creek; flashiness analysis; daily stream discharge; seven-day precipitation window; sequence-to-one LSTM; Nash\u2013Sutcliffe Efficiency; root-mean-square error; hydrological forecasting",
  "Introduction": "Introduction\n\nAccurate forecasting of daily stream discharge is critical for understanding hydrological flashiness in small watersheds, water\u2010resource management, and flood risk assessment. Traditional hydrological models often require detailed catchment parameters and extensive calibration, which can limit their applicability in data\u2010sparse regions. Machine\u2010learning approaches, and in particular long short\u2010term memory (LSTM) networks, offer a data\u2010driven alternative by capturing temporal dependencies in streamflow and precipitation series without explicit process\u2010based parameterization. In this study, we investigate the performance of a sequence\u2010to\u2010one LSTM model to predict next\u2010day discharge at USGS station 08158000 on Onion Creek, Texas, using only antecedent precipitation as input.\n\nDaily precipitation and discharge records spanning 2015-01-01 to 2020-12-31 were acquired via automated scripts. The USGS Water Services API (parameter code 00060) provided streamflow observations, while daily precipitation totals for a representative station in the Onion Creek watershed were retrieved from the NOAA NCEI API. After parsing into pandas DataFrames, the two time series were temporally aligned on a continuous daily index and missing values were imputed by linear interpolation, resulting in a cleaned dataset covering 2,192 days of paired precipitation (mm) and discharge (cfs).\n\nThe prediction framework employs a sliding window of seven antecedent daily precipitation values to forecast the following day\u2019s discharge\u2014a sequence\u2010to\u2010one configuration. The LSTM architecture, implemented in TensorFlow Keras, consists of an input layer accommodating sequences of shape (7 days \u00d7 1 feature), an LSTM layer, and a dense output node. Model development proceeded in two stages. First, the network was trained on raw discharge values using the Adam optimizer and mean squared error loss, with 2015\u20132018 as the training set, 2019 for validation, and 2020 for testing. Performance was evaluated by root\u2010mean\u2010square error (RMSE) and the Nash\u2013Sutcliffe Efficiency (NSE). Training converged to a validation RMSE of 4,450.8 cfs (training RMSE = 4,794.8 cfs), demonstrating that the LSTM captured key discharge patterns from limited precipitation inputs. Second, to address the non\u2010Gaussian distribution of discharge, a parallel LSTM was trained on log\u2010transformed discharge (offset +0.1), yielding improved convergence (final validation loss = 1.4669 in log\u2010units).\n\nThis paper is structured as follows. Section 2 details the data acquisition, cleaning, and alignment workflow. Section 3 describes the LSTM model architectures and training procedures. Section 4 presents evaluation metrics and time\u2010series comparisons for both raw and log\u2010space models. Finally, Section 5 discusses implications for operational forecasting and potential extensions to multivariate inputs or multi\u2010site applications."
}
