details.raw_content:
Results

Model Performance: Raw‐Space LSTM  
The sequence‐to‐one LSTM trained on raw discharge values converged after 50 epochs, achieving a training RMSE of 4 794.8 cfs and a validation RMSE of 4 450.8 cfs. On the independent test set (2020), evaluation using the hydrostats package (outputs/metrics.json) yielded a Nash–Sutcliffe Efficiency (NSE) of 0.58 and an RMSE of 4 520 cfs. These results indicate that, while the model captures the general temporal dynamics of Onion Creek discharge, its predictive accuracy on extreme flow events remains limited.

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{outputs/plot_initial.png}
    \caption{Time‐series comparison of observed versus LSTM‐predicted daily discharge (cfs) for the 2020 test period. The model reproduces the overall hydrograph shape but underestimates peak flows.}
\end{figure}

Model Performance: Log‐Space LSTM  
To address residual bias and non‐Gaussian discharge distribution, a parallel LSTM was trained on ln(discharge_cfs + 0.1). This log‐space model achieved a final validation loss of 1.4669 (log‐units). When back‐transformed and evaluated on the 2020 test set (outputs/log_metrics.json), the log‐space model attained an NSE of 0.63 and an RMSE of 3 980 cfs—surpassing the predefined performance threshold (NSE ≥ 0.6).

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{outputs/plot_log.png}
    \caption{Time‐series comparison of observed versus log‐space LSTM‐predicted daily discharge (cfs) for the 2020 test period. Log‐transformation improves prediction of both low‐flow and high‐flow conditions.}
\end{figure}

Comparison and Implications  
The log‐transformed LSTM demonstrates a clear improvement in both NSE and RMSE on the test dataset, reducing bias in peak‐flow estimation and enhancing overall flashiness characterization. These findings underscore the value of variance‐stabilizing transformations in LSTM‐based streamflow forecasting when using limited precipitation inputs.

