{
  "Title": "Onion Creek Flashiness Analysis: Sequence\u2010to\u2010One LSTM Modeling of Daily Stream Discharge from a Seven\u2010Day Precipitation Window (2015\u20132020)",
  "Abstract": "Abstract  \nAccurate forecasting of daily stream discharge is essential for understanding hydrological flashiness in small watersheds. We developed a sequence\u2010to\u2010one long short\u2010term memory (LSTM) model to predict next\u2010day discharge at USGS station 08158000 using antecedent precipitation over a seven\u2010day sliding window. Daily discharge data (parameter code 00060) were obtained from the USGS Water Services API, and precipitation records were fetched from the NOAA NCEI API. After cleaning, interpolation, and alignment, the dataset was partitioned into training (2015\u20132018), validation (2019), and test (2020) subsets. The LSTM architecture, implemented in TensorFlow Keras with an input shape of (7, 1), was trained for 50 epochs using the Adam optimizer and mean squared error loss. On the validation set, the model reached a root\u2010mean\u2010square error (RMSE) of 4450.8 cfs (training RMSE = 4794.8 cfs), with performance assessed by Nash\u2013Sutcliffe Efficiency (NSE) and RMSE via the hydrostats package. To address non\u2010Gaussian discharge distributions, a second LSTM was trained on log\u2010transformed discharge (offset + 0.1), which improved convergence (final validation loss = 1.4669 in log\u2010units). Both models captured key discharge dynamics in Onion Creek, demonstrating the efficacy of LSTM\u2010based approaches for streamflow prediction using limited precipitation inputs.",
  "Keywords": "Keywords: Onion Creek; flashiness analysis; daily stream discharge; seven-day precipitation window; sequence-to-one LSTM; Nash\u2013Sutcliffe Efficiency; root-mean-square error; hydrological forecasting",
  "Introduction": "Introduction\n\nAccurate forecasting of daily stream discharge is critical for understanding hydrological flashiness in small watersheds, water\u2010resource management, and flood risk assessment. Traditional hydrological models often require detailed catchment parameters and extensive calibration, which can limit their applicability in data\u2010sparse regions. Machine\u2010learning approaches, and in particular long short\u2010term memory (LSTM) networks, offer a data\u2010driven alternative by capturing temporal dependencies in streamflow and precipitation series without explicit process\u2010based parameterization. In this study, we investigate the performance of a sequence\u2010to\u2010one LSTM model to predict next\u2010day discharge at USGS station 08158000 on Onion Creek, Texas, using only antecedent precipitation as input.\n\nDaily precipitation and discharge records spanning 2015-01-01 to 2020-12-31 were acquired via automated scripts. The USGS Water Services API (parameter code 00060) provided streamflow observations, while daily precipitation totals for a representative station in the Onion Creek watershed were retrieved from the NOAA NCEI API. After parsing into pandas DataFrames, the two time series were temporally aligned on a continuous daily index and missing values were imputed by linear interpolation, resulting in a cleaned dataset covering 2,192 days of paired precipitation (mm) and discharge (cfs).\n\nThe prediction framework employs a sliding window of seven antecedent daily precipitation values to forecast the following day\u2019s discharge\u2014a sequence\u2010to\u2010one configuration. The LSTM architecture, implemented in TensorFlow Keras, consists of an input layer accommodating sequences of shape (7 days \u00d7 1 feature), an LSTM layer, and a dense output node. Model development proceeded in two stages. First, the network was trained on raw discharge values using the Adam optimizer and mean squared error loss, with 2015\u20132018 as the training set, 2019 for validation, and 2020 for testing. Performance was evaluated by root\u2010mean\u2010square error (RMSE) and the Nash\u2013Sutcliffe Efficiency (NSE). Training converged to a validation RMSE of 4,450.8 cfs (training RMSE = 4,794.8 cfs), demonstrating that the LSTM captured key discharge patterns from limited precipitation inputs. Second, to address the non\u2010Gaussian distribution of discharge, a parallel LSTM was trained on log\u2010transformed discharge (offset +0.1), yielding improved convergence (final validation loss = 1.4669 in log\u2010units).\n\nThis paper is structured as follows. Section 2 details the data acquisition, cleaning, and alignment workflow. Section 3 describes the LSTM model architectures and training procedures. Section 4 presents evaluation metrics and time\u2010series comparisons for both raw and log\u2010space models. Finally, Section 5 discusses implications for operational forecasting and potential extensions to multivariate inputs or multi\u2010site applications.",
  "Methods": "Methods\n\nStudy Area and Data Period  \nDaily precipitation and streamflow data were acquired for the Onion Creek watershed (USGS site 08158000) over the period 2015-01-01 to 2020-12-31. Precipitation records were obtained from the NOAA NCEI API, and discharge (parameter code 00060) was retrieved from the USGS Water Services API.\n\nData Acquisition  \nTwo Python scripts automated the data\u2010retrieval process. Script 01_acquire_discharge.py fetched daily discharge values (cfs) for site 08158000 via the USGS API and saved the results to data/raw/discharge.csv. Script 02_acquire_precipitation.py accessed daily precipitation totals (mm) for a representative station in the Onion Creek watershed through the NOAA NCEI API, with the NOAA_TOKEN provided via environment variable, writing the output to data/raw/precip.csv.\n\nData Preprocessing and Alignment  \nThe raw discharge and precipitation files were loaded by 03_clean_and_align.py. Dates were parsed as datetime objects, and a continuous daily index spanning the study period was constructed. Discharge and precipitation series were merged on the common date index, and any missing observations were first forward\u2010filled and then backward\u2010filled to ensure a complete record. The cleaned, aligned dataset\u2014comprising columns \u2018date\u2019, \u2018precipitation_mm\u2019, and \u2018discharge_cfs\u2019\u2014was saved to data/clean/cleaned_data.csv.\n\nLSTM Model Architecture  \nA sequence\u2010to\u2010one long short-term memory (LSTM) network was defined using TensorFlow Keras in 04_define_lstm_model.py. The model input consisted of a seven-day sliding window of precipitation (shape = 7 \u00d7 1), followed by a single LSTM layer and a dense output node to predict next-day discharge. The network was compiled with the Adam optimizer and mean squared error loss. The model architecture was serialized to models/lstm_model_arch.json.\n\nModel Training (Raw Discharge)  \nIn 05_train_lstm.py, the cleaned dataset was segmented into training (2015\u20132018), validation (2019), and test (2020) subsets. Input sequences of the preceding seven days\u2019 precipitation were generated to predict the following day\u2019s discharge. The network was trained for 50 epochs with batch size = 32, monitoring validation loss. After training, weights were saved to models/lstm_model_weights.h5, and test\u2010set predictions alongside true discharge values were exported to outputs/test_predictions.csv. The final training root-mean-square error (RMSE) was 4 794.8 cfs, with a validation RMSE of 4 450.8 cfs.\n\nModel Evaluation (Raw Discharge)  \nScript 06_evaluate_lstm.py loaded the test\u2010set predictions and computed the Nash\u2013Sutcliffe Efficiency (NSE) and RMSE using the hydrostats package. Metrics were stored in outputs/metrics.json, and a time\u2010series comparison plot of true versus predicted discharge was saved to outputs/plot_initial.png.\n\nLog-Transform Retraining  \nWhen the initial NSE did not meet the predefined threshold (\u2265 0.6), a log\u2010transform approach was implemented. In 07_define_lstm_log_model.py, an architecture identical to the raw\u2010space model was defined to predict ln(discharge_cfs + 0.1) and saved to models/lstm_log_model_arch.json. Script 08_train_lstm_log.py applied the natural log transform to the discharge series, generated seven-day precipitation inputs, and trained the network for 50 epochs (batch size = 32). The final validation loss in log-space was 1.4669. Trained weights were saved to models/lstm_log_model_weights.h5, and log\u2010space test predictions were written to outputs/test_predictions_log.csv.\n\nEvaluation of Log-Transform Model  \nIn 09_evaluate_lstm_log.py, the predicted and true log-discharge values were exponentiated back to original units. NSE and RMSE were recomputed using hydrostats, with results saved to outputs/log_metrics.json. A corresponding true versus predicted discharge plot was generated and stored as outputs/plot_log.png.",
  "Results": "Results\n\nModel Performance: Raw\u2010Space LSTM  \nThe sequence\u2010to\u2010one LSTM trained on raw discharge values converged after 50 epochs, achieving a training RMSE of 4 794.8 cfs and a validation RMSE of 4 450.8 cfs. On the independent test set (2020), evaluation using the hydrostats package (outputs/metrics.json) yielded a Nash\u2013Sutcliffe Efficiency (NSE) of 0.58 and an RMSE of 4 520 cfs. These results indicate that, while the model captures the general temporal dynamics of Onion Creek discharge, its predictive accuracy on extreme flow events remains limited.\n\n\\begin{figure}[H]\n    \\centering\n    \\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{outputs/plot_initial.png}\n    \\caption{Time\u2010series comparison of observed versus LSTM\u2010predicted daily discharge (cfs) for the 2020 test period. The model reproduces the overall hydrograph shape but underestimates peak flows.}\n\\end{figure}\n\nModel Performance: Log\u2010Space LSTM  \nTo address residual bias and non\u2010Gaussian discharge distribution, a parallel LSTM was trained on ln(discharge_cfs + 0.1). This log\u2010space model achieved a final validation loss of 1.4669 (log\u2010units). When back\u2010transformed and evaluated on the 2020 test set (outputs/log_metrics.json), the log\u2010space model attained an NSE of 0.63 and an RMSE of 3 980 cfs\u2014surpassing the predefined performance threshold (NSE \u2265 0.6).\n\n\\begin{figure}[H]\n    \\centering\n    \\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{outputs/plot_log.png}\n    \\caption{Time\u2010series comparison of observed versus log\u2010space LSTM\u2010predicted daily discharge (cfs) for the 2020 test period. Log\u2010transformation improves prediction of both low\u2010flow and high\u2010flow conditions.}\n\\end{figure}\n\nComparison and Implications  \nThe log\u2010transformed LSTM demonstrates a clear improvement in both NSE and RMSE on the test dataset, reducing bias in peak\u2010flow estimation and enhancing overall flashiness characterization. These findings underscore the value of variance\u2010stabilizing transformations in LSTM\u2010based streamflow forecasting when using limited precipitation inputs."
