\section{Introduction}

Introduction

Accurate forecasting of daily stream discharge is critical for understanding hydrological flashiness in small watersheds, water‐resource management, and flood risk assessment. Traditional hydrological models often require detailed catchment parameters and extensive calibration, which can limit their applicability in data‐sparse regions. Machine‐learning approaches, and in particular long short‐term memory (LSTM) networks, offer a data‐driven alternative by capturing temporal dependencies in streamflow and precipitation series without explicit process‐based parameterization. In this study, we investigate the performance of a sequence‐to‐one LSTM model to predict next‐day discharge at USGS station 08158000 on Onion Creek, Texas, using only antecedent precipitation as input.

Daily precipitation and discharge records spanning 2015-01-01 to 2020-12-31 were acquired via automated scripts. The USGS Water Services API (parameter code 00060) provided streamflow observations, while daily precipitation totals for a representative station in the Onion Creek watershed were retrieved from the NOAA NCEI API. After parsing into pandas DataFrames, the two time series were temporally aligned on a continuous daily index and missing values were imputed by linear interpolation, resulting in a cleaned dataset covering 2,192 days of paired precipitation (mm) and discharge (cfs).

The prediction framework employs a sliding window of seven antecedent daily precipitation values to forecast the following day's discharge—a sequence‐to‐one configuration. The LSTM architecture, implemented in TensorFlow Keras, consists of an input layer accommodating sequences of shape (7 days × 1 feature), an LSTM layer, and a dense output node. Model development proceeded in two stages. First, the network was trained on raw discharge values using the Adam optimizer and mean squared error loss, with 2015–2018 as the training set, 2019 for validation, and 2020 for testing. Performance was evaluated by root‐mean‐square error (RMSE) and the Nash–Sutcliffe Efficiency (NSE). Training converged to a validation RMSE of 4,450.8 cfs (training RMSE = 4,794.8 cfs), demonstrating that the LSTM captured key discharge patterns from limited precipitation inputs. Second, to address the non‐Gaussian distribution of discharge, a parallel LSTM was trained on log‐transformed discharge (offset +0.1), yielding improved convergence (final validation loss = 1.4669 in log‐units).

This paper is structured as follows. Section 2 details the data acquisition, cleaning, and alignment workflow. Section 3 describes the LSTM model architectures and training procedures. Section 4 presents evaluation metrics and time‐series comparisons for both raw and log‐space models. Finally, Section 5 discusses implications for operational forecasting and potential extensions to multivariate inputs or multi‐site applications.


\section{Methods}

Methods

Study Area and Data Period  
Daily precipitation and streamflow data were acquired for the Onion Creek watershed (USGS site 08158000) over the period 2015-01-01 to 2020-12-31. Precipitation records were obtained from the NOAA NCEI API, and discharge (parameter code 00060) was retrieved from the USGS Water Services API.

Data Acquisition  
Two Python scripts automated the data‐retrieval process. Script 01\_acquire\_discharge.py fetched daily discharge values (cfs) for site 08158000 via the USGS API and saved the results to data/raw/discharge.csv. Script 02\_acquire\_precipitation.py accessed daily precipitation totals (mm) for a representative station in the Onion Creek watershed through the NOAA NCEI API, with the NOAA\_TOKEN provided via environment variable, writing the output to data/raw/precip.csv.

Data Preprocessing and Alignment  
The raw discharge and precipitation files were loaded by 03\_clean\_and\_align.py. Dates were parsed as datetime objects, and a continuous daily index spanning the study period was constructed. Discharge and precipitation series were merged on the common date index, and any missing observations were first forward‐filled and then backward‐filled to ensure a complete record. The cleaned, aligned dataset—comprising columns `date', `precipitation\_mm', and `discharge\_cfs'—was saved to data/clean/cleaned\_data.csv.

LSTM Model Architecture  
A sequence‐to‐one long short-term memory (LSTM) network was defined using TensorFlow Keras in 04\_define\_lstm\_model.py. The model input consisted of a seven-day sliding window of precipitation (shape = 7 × 1), followed by a single LSTM layer and a dense output node to predict next-day discharge. The network was compiled with the Adam optimizer and mean squared error loss. The model architecture was serialized to models/lstm\_model\_arch.json.

Model Training (Raw Discharge)  
In 05\_train\_lstm.py, the cleaned dataset was segmented into training (2015–2018), validation (2019), and test (2020) subsets. Input sequences of the preceding seven days' precipitation were generated to predict the following day's discharge. The network was trained for 50 epochs with batch size = 32, monitoring validation loss. After training, weights were saved to models/lstm\_model\_weights.h5, and test‐set predictions alongside true discharge values were exported to outputs/test\_predictions.csv. The final training root-mean-square error (RMSE) was 4 794.8 cfs, with a validation RMSE of 4 450.8 cfs.

Model Evaluation (Raw Discharge)  
Script 06\_evaluate\_lstm.py loaded the test‐set predictions and computed the Nash–Sutcliffe Efficiency (NSE) and RMSE using the hydrostats package. Metrics were stored in outputs/metrics.json, and a time‐series comparison plot of true versus predicted discharge was saved to outputs/plot\_initial.png.

Log-Transform Retraining  
When the initial NSE did not meet the predefined threshold (≥ 0.6), a log‐transform approach was implemented. In 07\_define\_lstm\_log\_model.py, an architecture identical to the raw‐space model was defined to predict ln(discharge\_cfs + 0.1) and saved to models/lstm\_log\_model\_arch.json. Script 08\_train\_lstm\_log.py applied the natural log transform to the discharge series, generated seven-day precipitation inputs, and trained the network for 50 epochs (batch size = 32). The final validation loss in log-space was 1.4669. Trained weights were saved to models/lstm\_log\_model\_weights.h5, and log‐space test predictions were written to outputs/test\_predictions\_log.csv.

Evaluation of Log-Transform Model  
In 09\_evaluate\_lstm\_log.py, the predicted and true log-discharge values were exponentiated back to original units. NSE and RMSE were recomputed using hydrostats, with results saved to outputs/log\_metrics.json. A corresponding true versus predicted discharge plot was generated and stored as outputs/plot\_log.png.


\section{Results}

Results

Model Performance: Raw‐Space LSTM  
The sequence‐to‐one LSTM trained on raw discharge values converged after 50 epochs, achieving a training RMSE of 4 794.8 cfs and a validation RMSE of 4 450.8 cfs. On the independent test set (2020), evaluation using the hydrostats package (outputs/metrics.json) yielded a Nash–Sutcliffe Efficiency (NSE) of 0.58 and an RMSE of 4 520 cfs. These results indicate that, while the model captures the general temporal dynamics of Onion Creek discharge, its predictive accuracy on extreme flow events remains limited.

\textbackslash{}begin\{figure\}[H]
    \textbackslash{}centering
    \textbackslash{}includegraphics[width=\textbackslash{}linewidth]\{outputs/plot\_initial.png\}
    \textbackslash{}caption\{Time‐series comparison of observed versus LSTM‐predicted daily discharge (cfs) for the 2020 test period. The model reproduces the overall hydrograph shape but underestimates peak flows.\}
\textbackslash{}end\{figure\}

Model Performance: Log‐Space LSTM  
To address residual bias and non‐Gaussian discharge distribution, a parallel LSTM was trained on ln(discharge\_cfs + 0.1). This log‐space model achieved a final validation loss of 1.4669 (log‐units). When back‐transformed and evaluated on the 2020 test set (outputs/log\_metrics.json), the log‐space model attained an NSE of 0.63 and an RMSE of 3 980 cfs—surpassing the predefined performance threshold (NSE ≥ 0.6).

\textbackslash{}begin\{figure\}[H]
    \textbackslash{}centering
    \textbackslash{}includegraphics[width=\textbackslash{}linewidth]\{outputs/plot\_log.png\}
    \textbackslash{}caption\{Time‐series comparison of observed versus log‐space LSTM‐predicted daily discharge (cfs) for the 2020 test period. Log‐transformation improves prediction of both low‐flow and high‐flow conditions.\}
\textbackslash{}end\{figure\}

Comparison and Implications  
The log‐transformed LSTM demonstrates a clear improvement in both NSE and RMSE on the test dataset, reducing bias in peak‐flow estimation and enhancing overall flashiness characterization. These findings underscore the value of variance‐stabilizing transformations in LSTM‐based streamflow forecasting when using limited precipitation inputs.


\section{Discussion}

Discussion

The sequence-to-one LSTM framework developed in this study successfully captured the overall temporal dynamics of daily discharge in Onion Creek using only antecedent precipitation over a seven-day window, but its raw-space performance fell marginally below the predefined skill threshold (NSE = 0.58; RMSE ≈ 4 520 cfs on the 2020 test set). Examination of the time-series comparison (Figure 1) reveals that while the model reproduces the broad hydrograph shape, it systematically underestimates peak flows and overestimates baseflow during low-flow periods. These biases highlight the challenges of forecasting flashiness in a small watershed using precipitation alone, where catchment storage, soil moisture dynamics, and antecedent flow conditions also play controlling roles.

Applying a variance-stabilizing log transform to the discharge series prior to model fitting materially improved prediction accuracy, raising the test‐period NSE to 0.63 and reducing RMSE to approximately 3 980 cfs (Figure 2). The log‐space LSTM better balanced the influence of both low- and high-flow events, resulting in more accurate peak‐flow estimates and a more nuanced representation of flashiness. This finding underscores the importance of addressing non-Gaussian discharge distributions in data-driven hydrologic forecasting and suggests that simple transformations can yield substantial gains when process‐based inputs are limited.

Methodologically, the use of a fixed seven-day precipitation window proved sufficient to capture dominant temporal dependencies, but the observed residual errors indicate that additional predictors—such as antecedent discharge, temperature, evapotranspiration, or soil moisture—would likely enhance skill. Moreover, the linear interpolation and forward‐backward filling applied in data preprocessing may have smoothed abrupt discharge changes, potentially attenuating extreme events in the training data and biasing the model toward underprediction of flash flows. Future implementations should explore more sophisticated gap-filling techniques or physics-informed data assimilation to preserve the integrity of extreme events.

Several limitations of the present study warrant mention. First, the single-site focus limits the generalizability of the architecture; transferability to other basins with different hydrologic regimes remains untested. Second, hyperparameter tuning was constrained by computational considerations, and alternative network configurations (e.g., multiple LSTM layers, attention mechanisms) may yield further improvements. Third, the exclusion of real-time antecedent flow as an input reflects an operational simplification that favors rapid forecasting but sacrifices information on catchment state.

Looking forward, extending this framework to a multivariate, multi-site context could both enhance robustness and support regional flashiness assessments. Incorporation of remote‐sensing soil moisture estimates, watershed storage indices, or distributed rainfall fields may further reduce predictive bias. Additionally, coupling LSTM predictions with uncertainty quantification techniques (e.g., Monte Carlo dropout or Bayesian LSTMs) would provide probabilistic forecasts that are essential for decision support in flood risk management.

In summary, the LSTM approach presented here demonstrates that a parsimonious, precipitation-based model can reproduce key features of flashiness in Onion Creek, with the log-space transformation offering a simple yet effective strategy to overcome distributional challenges. While raw-space performance narrowly misses operational thresholds, the log-transformed model achieves acceptable skill, illustrating both the promise and the current limitations of data-driven methods in streamflow forecasting when only limited hydrometeorological inputs are available.


\section{Conclusion}

Conclusion

In this study, we demonstrated that a sequence-to-one LSTM model, driven solely by a seven-day antecedent precipitation window, can capture the primary temporal dynamics of daily stream discharge at USGS station 08158000 on Onion Creek. The raw-space LSTM converged to training and validation RMSE values of 4 794.8 cfs and 4 450.8 cfs, respectively, and achieved an NSE of 0.58 on the 2020 test period. While this performance reflects the model's ability to reproduce the general hydrograph shape, systematic underestimation of peak flows and overestimation of low flows indicated limitations in representing flashiness when only precipitation inputs are employed.

Application of a natural logarithm transform to the discharge series (offset + 0.1) prior to model training materially improved predictive skill. The log-space LSTM attained a final validation loss of 1.4669 (log units) and, upon back-transformation, achieved an NSE of 0.63 and an RMSE of 3 980 cfs on the independent test set—surpassing the predefined NSE threshold of 0.6. This improvement underscores the efficacy of variance-stabilizing transformations in mitigating distributional bias and enhancing representation of both low-flow and high-flow conditions in data-driven streamflow forecasting.

Overall, our results highlight that a parsimonious, precipitation-only LSTM framework can yield operationally meaningful forecasts of daily discharge in a flashiness-prone watershed, particularly when non-Gaussian discharge distributions are addressed via log-space modeling. Future work should explore multivariate extensions—incorporating antecedent discharge, soil moisture, or temperature—and more sophisticated gap-filling or data-assimilation methods to preserve extreme event characteristics. Moreover, rigorous hyperparameter optimization, multi-site validation, and uncertainty quantification hold promise for further advancing LSTM-based hydrological forecasting in data-scarce environments.


