To address residual bias and non-Gaussian discharge distribution, a parallel LSTM was trained on $\ln(\mathrm{discharge\_cfs}+0.1)$. This log-space model achieved a final validation loss of 1.4669 (log units). When back-transformed and evaluated on the 2020 test set (\texttt{outputs/log\_metrics.json}), the log-space model attained an NSE of 0.63 and an RMSE of 3,980~cfs—surpassing the predefined performance threshold (NSE $\geq0.6$).

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{outputs/plot_log.png}
\caption{Time-series comparison of observed versus log-space LSTM-predicted daily discharge (cfs) for the 2020 test period. Log-transformation improves prediction of both low-flow and high-flow conditions.}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Comparison and Implications}
The log-transformed LSTM demonstrates a clear improvement in both NSE and RMSE on the test dataset, reducing bias in peak-flow estimation and enhancing overall flashiness characterization. These findings underscore the value of variance-stabilizing transformations in LSTM-based streamflow forecasting when using limited precipitation inputs.

\section{Discussion}

The sequence-to-one LSTM framework developed in this study successfully captured the overall temporal dynamics of daily discharge in Onion Creek using only antecedent precipitation over a seven-day window, but its raw-space performance fell marginally below the predefined skill threshold (NSE = 0.58; RMSE $\approx$ 4,520~cfs on the 2020 test set). Examination of the time-series comparison (Figure~1) reveals that while the model reproduces the broad hydrograph shape, it systematically underestimates peak flows and overestimates baseflow during low-flow periods. These biases highlight the challenges of forecasting flashiness in a small watershed using precipitation alone, where catchment storage, soil moisture dynamics, and antecedent flow conditions also play controlling roles.

Applying a variance-stabilizing log transform to the discharge series prior to model fitting materially improved prediction accuracy, raising the test-period NSE to 0.63 and reducing RMSE to approximately 3,980~cfs (Figure~2). The log-space LSTM better balanced the influence of both low- and high-flow events, resulting in more accurate peak-flow estimates and a more nuanced representation of flashiness. This finding underscores the importance of addressing non-Gaussian discharge distributions in data-driven hydrologic forecasting and suggests that simple transformations can yield substantial gains when process-based inputs are limited.

Methodologically, the use of a fixed seven-day precipitation window proved sufficient to capture dominant temporal dependencies, but the observed residual errors indicate that additional predictors—such as antecedent discharge, temperature, evapotranspiration, or soil moisture—would likely enhance skill. Moreover, the linear interpolation and forward-backward filling applied in data preprocessing may have smoothed abrupt discharge changes, potentially attenuating extreme events in the training data and biasing the model toward underprediction of flash flows. Future implementations should explore more sophisticated gap-filling techniques or physics-informed data assimilation to preserve the integrity of extreme events.

Several limitations of the present study warrant mention. First, the single-site focus limits the generalizability of the architecture; transferability to other basins with different hydrologic regimes remains untested. Second, hyperparameter tuning was constrained by computational considerations, and alternative network configurations (e.g., multiple LSTM layers, attention mechanisms) may yield further improvements. Third, the exclusion of real-time antecedent flow as an input reflects an operational simplification that favors rapid forecasting but sacrifices information on catchment state.

Looking forward, extending this framework to a multivariate, multi-site context could both enhance robustness and support regional flashiness assessments. Incorporation of remote-sensing soil moisture estimates, watershed storage indices, or distributed rainfall fields may further reduce predictive bias. Additionally, coupling LSTM predictions with uncertainty quantification techniques (e.g., Monte Carlo dropout or Bayesian LSTMs) would provide probabilistic forecasts that are essential for decision support in flood risk management.

In summary, the LSTM approach presented here demonstrates that a parsimonious, precipitation-based model can reproduce key features of flashiness in Onion Creek, with the log-space transformation offering a simple yet effective strategy to overcome distributional challenges. While raw-space performance narrowly misses operational thresholds, the log-transformed model achieves acceptable skill, illustrating both the promise and the current limitations of data-driven methods in streamflow forecasting when only limited hydrometeorological inputs are available.

\section{Conclusion}

In this study, we demonstrated that a sequence-to-one LSTM model, driven solely by a seven-day antecedent precipitation window, can capture the primary temporal dynamics of daily stream discharge at USGS station 08158000 on Onion Creek. The raw-space LSTM converged to training and validation RMSE values of 4,794.8~cfs and 4,450.8~cfs, respectively, and achieved an NSE of 0.58 on the 2020 test period. While this performance reflects the model's ability to reproduce the general hydrograph shape, systematic underestimation of peak flows and overestimation of low flows indicated limitations in representing flashiness when only precipitation inputs are employed.

Application of a natural logarithm transform to the discharge series (offset +0.1) prior to model training materially improved predictive skill. The log-space LSTM attained a final validation loss of 1.4669 (log units) and, upon back-transformation, achieved an NSE of 0.63 and an RMSE of 3,980~cfs on the independent test set—surpassing the predefined NSE threshold of 0.6. This improvement underscores the efficacy of variance-stabilizing transformations in mitigating distributional bias and enhancing representation of both low-flow and high-flow conditions in data-driven streamflow forecasting.

Overall, our results highlight that a parsimonious, precipitation-only LSTM framework can yield operationally meaningful forecasts of daily discharge in a flashiness-prone watershed, particularly when non-Gaussian discharge distributions are addressed via log-space modeling. Future work should explore multivariate extensions—incorporating antecedent discharge, soil moisture, or temperature—and more sophisticated gap-filling or data-assimilation methods to preserve extreme event characteristics. Moreover, rigorous hyperparameter optimization, multi-site validation, and uncertainty quantification hold promise for further advancing LSTM-based hydrological forecasting in data-scarce environments.

