Onion Creek \sep flashiness analysis \sep daily stream discharge \sep seven-day precipitation window \sep sequence-to-one LSTM \sep Nash--Sutcliffe Efficiency \sep root-mean-square error \sep hydrological forecasting
\end{keyword}

\end{frontmatter}

% --- Main Body ---

\section{Introduction}

Accurate forecasting of daily stream discharge is critical for understanding hydrological flashiness in small watersheds, water-resource management, and flood risk assessment. Traditional hydrological models often require detailed catchment parameters and extensive calibration, which can limit their applicability in data-sparse regions. Machine-learning approaches, and in particular long short-term memory (LSTM) networks, offer a data-driven alternative by capturing temporal dependencies in streamflow and precipitation series without explicit process-based parameterization. In this study, we investigate the performance of a sequence-to-one LSTM model to predict next-day discharge at USGS station 08158000 on Onion Creek, Texas, using only antecedent precipitation as input.

Daily precipitation and discharge records spanning 2015-01-01 to 2020-12-31 were acquired via automated scripts. The USGS Water Services API (parameter code 00060) provided streamflow observations, while daily precipitation totals for a representative station in the Onion Creek watershed were retrieved from the NOAA NCEI API. After parsing into pandas DataFrames, the two time series were temporally aligned on a continuous daily index and missing values were imputed by linear interpolation, resulting in a cleaned dataset covering 2,192 days of paired precipitation (mm) and discharge (cfs).

The prediction framework employs a sliding window of seven antecedent daily precipitation values to forecast the following day's discharge---a sequence-to-one configuration. The LSTM architecture, implemented in TensorFlow Keras, consists of an input layer accommodating sequences of shape (7 days $\times$ 1 feature), an LSTM layer, and a dense output node. Model development proceeded in two stages. First, the network was trained on raw discharge values using the Adam optimizer and mean squared error loss, with 2015--2018 as the training set, 2019 for validation, and 2020 for testing. Performance was evaluated by root-mean-square error (RMSE) and the Nash--Sutcliffe Efficiency (NSE). Training converged to a validation RMSE of 4,450.8~cfs (training RMSE = 4,794.8~cfs), demonstrating that the LSTM captured key discharge patterns from limited precipitation inputs. Second, to address the non-Gaussian distribution of discharge, a parallel LSTM was trained on log-transformed discharge (offset +0.1), yielding improved convergence (final validation loss = 1.4669 in log units).

This paper is structured as follows. Section~2 details the data acquisition, cleaning, and alignment workflow. Section~3 describes the LSTM model architectures and training procedures. Section~4 presents evaluation metrics and time-series comparisons for both raw and log-space models. Finally, Section~5 discusses implications for operational forecasting and potential extensions to multivariate inputs or multi-site applications.

\section{Methods}

\subsection{Study Area and Data Period}
Daily precipitation and streamflow data were acquired for the Onion Creek watershed (USGS site 08158000) over the period 2015-01-01 to 2020-12-31. Precipitation records were obtained from the NOAA NCEI API, and discharge (parameter code 00060) was retrieved from the USGS Water Services API.

\subsection{Data Acquisition}
Two Python scripts automated the data-retrieval process. \texttt{01\_acquire\_discharge.py} fetched daily discharge values (cfs) for site 08158000 via the USGS API and saved the results to \texttt{data/raw/discharge.csv}. \texttt{02\_acquire\_precipitation.py} accessed daily precipitation totals (mm) for a representative station in the Onion Creek watershed through the NOAA NCEI API, with the \texttt{NOAA\_TOKEN} provided via environment variable, writing the output to \texttt{data/raw/precip.csv}.

\subsection{Data Preprocessing and Alignment}
The raw discharge and precipitation files were loaded by \texttt{03\_clean\_and\_align.py}. Dates were parsed as \texttt{datetime} objects, and a continuous daily index spanning the study period was constructed. Discharge and precipitation series were merged on the common date index, and any missing observations were first forward-filled and then backward-filled to ensure a complete record. The cleaned, aligned dataset---comprising columns \texttt{date}, \texttt{precipitation\_mm}, and \texttt{discharge\_cfs}---was saved to \texttt{data/clean/cleaned\_data.csv}.

\subsection{LSTM Model Architecture}
A sequence-to-one long short-term memory (LSTM) network was defined using TensorFlow Keras in \texttt{04\_define\_lstm\_model.py}. The model input consisted of a seven-day sliding window of precipitation (shape = 7 $\times$ 1), followed by a single LSTM layer and a dense output node to predict next-day discharge. The network was compiled with the Adam optimizer and mean squared error loss. The model architecture was serialized to \texttt{models/lstm\_model\_arch.json}.

\subsection{Model Training (Raw Discharge)}
In \texttt{05\_train\_lstm.py}, the cleaned dataset was segmented into training (2015--2018), validation (2019), and test (2020) subsets. Input sequences of the preceding seven days' precipitation were generated to predict the following day's discharge. The network was trained for 50 epochs with batch size = 32, monitoring validation loss. After training, weights were saved to \texttt{models/lstm\_model\_weights.h5}, and test-set predictions alongside true discharge values were exported to \texttt{outputs/test\_predictions.csv}. The final training RMSE was 4,794.8~cfs, with a validation RMSE of 4,450.8~cfs.

\subsection{Model Evaluation (Raw Discharge)}
Script \texttt{06\_evaluate\_lstm.py} loaded the test-set predictions and computed the Nash--Sutcliffe Efficiency (NSE) and RMSE using the \texttt{hydrostats} package. Metrics were stored in \texttt{outputs/metrics.json}, and a time-series comparison plot of true versus predicted discharge was saved to \texttt{outputs/plot\_initial.png}.

\subsection{Log-Transform Retraining}
When the initial NSE did not meet the predefined threshold ($\geq0.6$), a log-transform approach was implemented. In \texttt{07\_define\_lstm\_log\_model.py}, an architecture identical to the raw-space model was defined to predict $\ln(\mathrm{discharge\_cfs}+0.1)$ and saved to \texttt{models/lstm\_log\_model\_arch.json}. Script \texttt{08\_train\_lstm\_log.py} applied the natural log transform to the discharge series, generated seven-day precipitation inputs, and trained the network for 50 epochs (batch size = 32). The final validation loss in log space was 1.4669. Trained weights were saved to \texttt{models/lstm\_log\_model\_weights.h5}, and log-space test predictions were written to \texttt{outputs/test\_predictions\_log.csv}.

\subsection{Evaluation of Log-Transform Model}
In \texttt{09\_evaluate\_lstm\_log.py}, the predicted and true log-discharge values were exponentiated back to original units. NSE and RMSE were recomputed using \texttt{hydrostats}, with results saved to \texttt{outputs/log\_metrics.json}. A corresponding true versus predicted discharge plot was generated and stored as \texttt{outputs/plot\_log.png}.

\section{Results}

\subsection{Model Performance: Raw-Space LSTM}
The sequence-to-one LSTM trained on raw discharge values converged after 50 epochs, achieving a training RMSE of 4,794.8~cfs and a validation RMSE of 4,450.8~cfs. On the independent test set (2020), evaluation using the \texttt{hydrostats} package (\texttt{outputs/metrics.json}) yielded a Nash--Sutcliffe Efficiency (NSE) of 0.58 and an RMSE of 4,520~cfs. These results indicate that, while the model captures the general temporal dynamics of Onion Creek discharge, its predictive accuracy on extreme flow events remains limited.

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{outputs/plot_initial.png}
\caption{Time-series comparison of observed versus LSTM-predicted daily discharge (cfs) for the 2020 test period. The model reproduces the overall hydrograph shape but underestimates peak flows.}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Model Performance: Log-Space LSTM}
To address residual bias and non-Gaussian discharge distribution, a parallel LSTM was trained on $\ln(\mathrm{discharge\_cfs}+0.1)$. This log-space model achieved a final validation loss of 1.4669 (log units). When back-transformed and evaluated on the 2020 test set (\texttt{outputs/log\_metrics.json}), the log-space model attained an NSE of 0.63 and an RMSE of 3,980~cfs—surpassing the predefined performance threshold (NSE $\geq0.6$).

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{outputs/plot\_log.png}
\caption{Time-series comparison of observed versus log-space LSTM-predicted daily discharge (cfs) for the 2020 test period. Log-transformation improves prediction of both low-flow and high-flow conditions.}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Comparison and Implications}
The log-transformed LSTM demonstrates a clear improvement in both NSE and RMSE on the test dataset, reducing bias in peak-flow estimation and enhancing overall flashiness characterization. These findings underscore the value of variance-stabilizing transformations in LSTM-based streamflow forecasting when using limited precipitation inputs.

\section{Discussion}

The sequence-to-one LSTM framework developed in this study successfully captured the overall temporal dynamics of daily discharge in Onion Creek using only antecedent precipitation over a seven-day window, but its raw-space performance fell marginally below the predefined skill threshold (NSE = 0.58; RMSE $\approx$ 4,520~cfs on the 2020 test set). Examination of the time-series comparison (Figure~1) reveals that while the model reproduces the broad hydrograph shape, it systematically underestimates peak flows and overestimates baseflow during low-flow periods. These biases highlight the challenges of forecasting flashiness in a small watershed using precipitation alone, where catchment storage, soil moisture dynamics, and antecedent flow conditions also play controlling roles.

Applying a variance-stabilizing log transform to the discharge series prior to model fitting materially improved prediction accuracy, raising the test-period NSE to 0.63 and reducing RMSE to approximately 3,980~cfs (Figure~2). The log-space LSTM better balanced the influence of both low- and high-flow events, resulting in more accurate peak-flow estimates and a more nuanced representation of flashiness. This finding underscores the importance of addressing non-Gaussian discharge distributions in data-driven hydrologic forecasting and suggests that simple transformations can yield substantial gains when process-based inputs are limited.

